														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2168294">
									

<blockquote>
<p>the point of this SCP, the Foundation, and writing in general</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Re-interpreting and incorporating SCPs is the whole point of writing. SCP itself is based of re-interpretations of the format of SCP-173. Writing in general is all about interpretating and re-interpreting whatever input happens. The first example that comes to mind is Laird Hunt, an author who is a finalist for the 2013 PEN/Faulkner Award for Fiction for his novel, Kind One.I've spoken with Laird Hunt and he's told me that Kind One was literally a re-interpretation of a throw-away conversation in another novel he read.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Complaining that the results of a demonic shop class don't match up with your personal beliefs</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm saying that for the initial idea, the execution is lackluster and frankly boring/uncreative. It's based on very stereotypical understandings of good and evil and really doesn't take the initial idea to any new heights. I'm here to be impressed and invested in something, even if it's an idea I've seen before. And given the initial idea and the execution, it's unimpressive and I feel it could've been executed in a far more clever fashion.</p>
<p>For example, it could've questioned the straight definition of a demon. Why is it an abrahamic demon academy? There are other &quot;demon&quot; like entities that aren't necessarily &quot;evil&quot; according to the stereotype perception of it. You could have a lot of fun representing a lot of ideas on what a demon is and what evil is and what a demonic pet project is, but here it's very laid out and exactly as expected given the thing.</p>

							</div>

										<div class="signature">
					<hr class="signature-separator"/>
					

<p>Living the dream, or dreaming the life?</p>

				</div>
					
