														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2168688">
									

<p>Personally, I find the writing gimmick of &quot;Here's a word everyone is familiar with and has a particular meaning. The hook is that I define it to mean the opposite of what everyone else does.&quot; to be a more convoluted and lazier version of an unreliable narrator. I mean, if you boil it down, it becomes &quot;The hook is I lied.&quot;</p>
<p>If the story has a character with horns, hooves, red skin, pointy tail, and smells of sulfur but is actually a nice creature that superficially looks like a demon, fine. (Although that specific example bumps right up on the line, it's intended as hyperbole.) If the story *has* a demon that, until the very end, confirms his demon status and then oops he's really not-demon, that's lazy writing.</p>
<p>But we're not talking about anything of this nature in this SCP. Up until the clock, it could've simply been evil gnomes or goblins, but the clock pretty clearly invokes &quot;hellish&quot; as a concept.</p>
<p>But my original post was not a response to you. It was a objection to the unnecessary insertion of politically-correct verbiage to a piece of writing, where the suggested change runs counter to the concept of the story, but promotes or indulges someone else's activism.</p>

							</div>

								
