																		It definitely needs the backstory
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ankhananku" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1964779); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1964779&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606401943" alt="AnkhAnanku" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1964779)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ankhananku" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1964779); return false;" >AnkhAnanku</a></span> <span class="odate time_1417208173 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Nov 2014 20:56</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2168204">
									

<p>Otherwise it just looks like your standard curio-shop-of-destiny and a list of unfinished SCPs that couldn't be bothered to flesh out. Maybe I wasn't paying attention, but I didn't catch the whole &quot;demonic shop class&quot; thing on the first go. I think I was distracted by the cliché Curio Shoppe frame story to appreciate the items and the grading system.</p>
<p>I actually really like how you've tried to come up with a better solution to the whole &quot;drug the populace&quot; thing and brought in some alternative reasoning. I like the idea of there actually being an Ethics Committee that's a bit more reasonable than what most of the Foundation is willing and ready to do.</p>
<p>This is actually a really neat concept, so to help out I've got some constructive criticism, if I may&#8230;</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">My&nbsp;Notes</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">chew&nbsp;on&nbsp;this&nbsp;a&nbsp;bit...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Might I suggest re-framing the description to better hint that the shop is really more of the door by which the anomalous items enter our world? Perhaps failed attempts to intercept the items or the owner before they make it to the shop&#8230;</p>
<p>Consider changing the description from &quot;various second-hand goods&quot; to something indicating them being crudely hand-crafted. &quot;Second-hand&quot; is kind of a red herring; it puts the reader in the frame of mind that these are items collected from various sources rather than a common origin and it does nothing to draw interest into the objects themselves.</p>
<p>Mr. Anziano is not a particularly demonic name. I know you can't spell it out, but to me it was just foreign, without malevolence. Still, it would work well if some attention were drawn away from establishing himself and the Shoppe as the focus of attention.</p>
<p>Footnote 2's should have left the effects of 2197-2 in Document 2197-101. Also consider relabeling the supplemental documentation (what if Mr. Anziano sells more than 100 items?)</p>
<p>2197-5 was an entertaining failure, but the reference was so spot on until the punch line that it looked like unimaginative writing.</p>
<p>2197-22 I feel like the physical description of the bookends could be more succinct.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Within a week of coloring in the page, the subject invariably meets an ideal romantic partner and enters into a long-term relationship.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At first I found the stated effects of 2197-25 a little too mushy, but in this sentence ran it from blandly positive into straight-up glurge. It read like one of those chain-mails that clutter up message boards, and I was too busy rolling my eyes to appreciate the trope-worthy escape incidents which followed.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

